351 0 obj
  << /ActualText (Dear Testing framework reading this\054\n\nA hungry Fox saw some fine bunches of Grapes hanging from a vine that was trained along a high trellis\054 and did his best to reach them by jumping as high as he could into the air\056 But it was all in vain\054 for they were just out of reach\072 so he gave up trying\054 and walked away with an air of dignity and unconcern\054 remarking\054 \042I thought those Grapes were ripe\054 but I see now they are quite sour\056\042\n\nA Man and his Wife had the good fortune to possess a Goose which laid a Golden Egg every day\056 Lucky though they were\054 they soon began to think they were not getting rich fast enough\054 and\054 imagining the bird must be made of gold inside\054 they decided to kill it in order to secure the whole store of precious metal at once\056 But when they cut it open they found it was just like any other goose\056 Thus\054 they neither got rich all at once\054 as they had hoped\054 nor enjoyed any longer the daily addition to their wealth\056\nMuch wants more and loses all\056\n\nThere was once a house that was overrun with Mice\056 A Cat heard of this\054 and said to herself\054 \042That\047s the place for me\054\042 and off she went and took up her quarters in the house\054 and caught the Mice one by one and ate them\056 At last the Mice could stand it no longer\054 and they determined to take to their holes and stay there\056 \042That\047s awkward\054\042 said the Cat to herself\072 \042the only thing to do is to coax them out by a trick\056\042 So she considered a while\054 and then climbed up the wall and let herself hang down by her hind legs from a peg\054 and pretended to be dead\056 By and by a Mouse peeped out and saw the Cat hanging there\056 \042Aha\041\042 it cried\054 \042you\047re very clever\054 madam\054 no doubt\072 but you may turn yourself into a bag of meal hanging there\054 if you like\054 yet you won\047t catch us coming anywhere near you\056\042\nIf you are wise you won\047t be deceived by the innocent airs of those whom you have once found to be dangerous\056\n\nThere was once a Dog who used to snap at people and bite them without any provocation\054 and who was a great nuisance to every one who came to his master\047s house\056 So his master fastened a bell round his neck to warn people of his presence\056 The Dog was very proud of the bell\054 and strutted about tinkling it with immense satisfaction\056 But an old dog came up to him and said\054 \042The fewer airs you give yourself the better\054 my friend\056 You don\047t think\054 do you\054 that your bell was given you as a reward of merit\077 On the contrary\054 it is a badge of disgrace\056\042\nNotoriety is often mistaken for fame\056\n\nThank you\054\nAESOP)
     /K [ 7 ]
     /P 352 0 R
     /Pg 337 0 R
     /S /Span
     /Type /StructElem
  >>
